1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,180
You've been told AI is why new grads are

2
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:03,850
struggling to find jobs.

3
00:00:03,890 --> 00:00:05,450
A New York Fed study found the trouble

4
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:08,026
started before ChatGPT existed.

5
00:00:08,376 --> 00:00:10,086
The common version goes like this.

6
00:00:10,126 --> 00:00:12,426
Companies adopted ChatGPT,

7
00:00:12,466 --> 00:00:13,886
junior work got automated,

8
00:00:13,926 --> 00:00:16,246
so the first rung of the career ladder disappeared.

9
00:00:16,286 --> 00:00:17,766
Videos telling that story have been

10
00:00:17,806 --> 00:00:19,371
watched millions of times.

11
00:00:19,491 --> 00:00:21,021
Young graduates are struggling.

12
00:00:21,061 --> 00:00:22,061
That part is true.

13
00:00:22,101 --> 00:00:22,901
The question is why,

14
00:00:22,901 --> 00:00:24,821
because the answer changes what a graduate

15
00:00:24,861 --> 00:00:25,847
should do about it.

16
00:00:25,967 --> 00:00:27,727
In June 2026,

17
00:00:27,767 --> 00:00:29,757
three economists published a study on the

18
00:00:29,797 --> 00:00:31,447
New York Fed's research blog.

19
00:00:31,487 --> 00:00:33,127
Their study looks at when the problem started,

20
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:35,091
and in which kinds of jobs.

21
00:00:35,211 --> 00:00:36,871
Their answer points at a change that came

22
00:00:36,911 --> 00:00:39,421
before AI tools were in wide use.

23
00:00:39,461 --> 00:00:40,581
A second study, from Stanford,

24
00:00:40,621 --> 00:00:42,948
then adds a newer problem on top.

25
00:00:44,298 --> 00:00:45,598
Start with the gap itself.

26
00:00:45,638 --> 00:00:47,878
The New York Fed tracks recent college graduates,

27
00:00:47,918 --> 00:00:49,948
aged 22 to 27.

28
00:00:49,988 --> 00:00:52,378
In the second quarter of 2026,

29
00:00:52,418 --> 00:00:55,378
their unemployment rate was about 5.6 percent.

30
00:00:55,418 --> 00:00:57,788
And 42 percent of them were underemployed,

31
00:00:57,828 --> 00:01:00,134
meaning they held a job that doesn't need a degree.

32
00:01:00,254 --> 00:01:01,934
For graduates under 29,

33
00:01:01,974 --> 00:01:04,514
the unemployment rate averaged about 3.1

34
00:01:04,554 --> 00:01:06,574
percent in the years before the pandemic.

35
00:01:06,614 --> 00:01:07,574
In the years after,

36
00:01:07,614 --> 00:01:09,874
it averaged about 3.7 percent,

37
00:01:09,914 --> 00:01:11,588
a rise of roughly a fifth.

38
00:01:11,708 --> 00:01:12,648
Over the same years,

39
00:01:12,688 --> 00:01:14,388
older graduates went the other way.

40
00:01:14,428 --> 00:01:16,034
Their rate edged down slightly.

41
00:01:16,154 --> 00:01:16,954
On its own,

42
00:01:16,574 --> 00:01:18,944
that pattern fits the AI explanation.

43
00:01:18,984 --> 00:01:21,134
Juniors get replaced while seniors stay.

44
00:01:21,174 --> 00:01:22,378
The researchers checked that.

45
00:01:23,228 --> 00:01:25,178
They sorted jobs into two groups.

46
00:01:25,218 --> 00:01:26,378
Jobs you can do from home,

47
00:01:26,418 --> 00:01:27,988
like software engineering.

48
00:01:28,028 --> 00:01:29,488
And jobs where you have to show up,

49
00:01:29,528 --> 00:01:31,233
like mechanical engineering.

50
00:01:31,353 --> 00:01:32,563
In the from-home jobs,

51
00:01:32,603 --> 00:01:35,395
unemployment for young graduates rose by

52
00:01:35,435 --> 00:01:36,623
about one percentage point between the

53
00:01:36,663 --> 00:01:41,013
pre-pandemic years and 2022 to 2024.

54
00:01:41,053 --> 00:01:43,863
Older workers in those same jobs saw a slight decline.

55
00:01:43,983 --> 00:01:45,263
In the show-up jobs,

56
00:01:45,303 --> 00:01:47,003
the gap between young and old widened

57
00:01:47,043 --> 00:01:48,823
briefly in 2020,

58
00:01:48,863 --> 00:01:50,297
then went back to where it had been.

59
00:01:50,417 --> 00:01:52,747
So the gap opened in the jobs that went remote,

60
00:01:52,787 --> 00:01:54,387
and it didn't stay open in the jobs that

61
00:01:54,427 --> 00:01:55,407
stayed in person.

62
00:01:55,447 --> 00:01:56,277
By the study's count,

63
00:01:56,317 --> 00:01:58,137
remote work has grown about four-fold

64
00:01:58,177 --> 00:01:59,207
since the pandemic.

65
00:01:59,247 --> 00:02:00,907
The timing points the same way.

66
00:02:00,947 --> 00:02:03,497
Youth unemployment had been rising since the pandemic,

67
00:02:03,537 --> 00:02:07,162
and ChatGPT only launched in November 2022.

68
00:02:07,512 --> 00:02:09,980
Here is why distance seems to hurt new hires most.

69
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:12,040
The researchers had internal data from one

70
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:13,950
Fortune 500 company.

71
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:15,580
When employees sat near each other,

72
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:18,410
they exchanged more feedback and more mentoring.

73
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:20,510
Even a short physical distance reduced that,

74
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:22,714
and it reduced it more for younger workers.

75
00:02:22,834 --> 00:02:24,524
When that company's offices closed,

76
00:02:24,564 --> 00:02:26,804
it hired fewer inexperienced workers.

77
00:02:26,844 --> 00:02:27,984
In the authors' words,

78
00:02:28,024 --> 00:02:29,944
a firm "shies away from employing

79
00:02:29,984 --> 00:02:32,384
inexperienced workers if distance creates

80
00:02:32,424 --> 00:02:34,317
barriers to training and development."

81
00:02:34,437 --> 00:02:35,697
Two cautions apply.

82
00:02:35,737 --> 00:02:37,937
The inside view comes from a single company,

83
00:02:37,977 --> 00:02:40,097
so it can't stand in for the whole economy.

84
00:02:40,137 --> 00:02:41,917
And the authors say remote work "can

85
00:02:41,957 --> 00:02:43,227
explain" the rise.

86
00:02:43,267 --> 00:02:44,087
That makes it an argument,

87
00:02:44,127 --> 00:02:45,740
and the case is still open.

88
00:02:46,090 --> 00:02:48,681
How much of the problem does remote work account for?

89
00:02:48,801 --> 00:02:50,381
The researchers ran what they call a

90
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:52,861
back-of-the-envelope calculation. Their estimate:

91
00:02:52,901 --> 00:02:55,521
remote work can explain about 64 percent

92
00:02:55,561 --> 00:02:57,041
of the recent increase in unemployment

93
00:02:57,081 --> 00:02:59,211
among young college graduates.

94
00:02:59,251 --> 00:03:00,943
So read it as a rough size.

95
00:03:01,063 --> 00:03:02,653
They also tested an objection.

96
00:03:02,693 --> 00:03:04,373
Maybe the from-home jobs are simply the

97
00:03:04,413 --> 00:03:06,283
same jobs AI is good at.

98
00:03:06,323 --> 00:03:08,733
So they held each occupation's AI exposure

99
00:03:08,773 --> 00:03:10,983
fixed and ran the comparison again.

100
00:03:11,023 --> 00:03:12,783
The gap between young and older workers

101
00:03:12,823 --> 00:03:13,747
was still there.

102
00:03:13,867 --> 00:03:15,017
That is the first problem.

103
00:03:15,057 --> 00:03:16,837
A second study looks at the years after

104
00:03:16,877 --> 00:03:19,336
ChatGPT and finds something else.

105
00:03:19,686 --> 00:03:20,707
In August 2026,

106
00:03:20,747 --> 00:03:23,456
Erik Brynjolfsson and two colleagues at

107
00:03:23,496 --> 00:03:25,676
Stanford updated a study based on payroll

108
00:03:25,716 --> 00:03:27,456
records from ADP.

109
00:03:27,496 --> 00:03:30,506
They focused on workers aged 22 to 25 in

110
00:03:30,546 --> 00:03:32,915
the occupations most exposed to AI.

111
00:03:33,035 --> 00:03:34,855
By June 2026,

112
00:03:34,895 --> 00:03:36,695
employment for those young workers was

113
00:03:36,735 --> 00:03:38,725
about 19 percent lower,

114
00:03:38,765 --> 00:03:41,265
relative to young workers in less exposed jobs.

115
00:03:41,305 --> 00:03:44,922
In July 2025, the same gap was 15 percent.

116
00:03:45,042 --> 00:03:46,722
The gap holds up when the authors control

117
00:03:46,762 --> 00:03:49,262
for interest rates and for remote work.

118
00:03:49,302 --> 00:03:50,722
Experienced workers in the same

119
00:03:50,762 --> 00:03:53,179
occupations show no comparable gap.

120
00:03:53,299 --> 00:03:54,699
The declines are concentrated in

121
00:03:54,739 --> 00:03:57,059
occupations where AI tends to do the task

122
00:03:57,099 --> 00:03:58,279
instead of the person.

123
00:03:58,319 --> 00:04:00,529
Where AI is mainly used to help workers,

124
00:04:00,569 --> 00:04:02,851
young employment is flat or rising.

125
00:04:02,971 --> 00:04:05,491
The Stanford authors attach their own cautions.

126
00:04:05,531 --> 00:04:07,231
They call these descriptive patterns,

127
00:04:07,271 --> 00:04:08,761
not causal estimates.

128
00:04:08,801 --> 00:04:10,021
Some of the differences appear before

129
00:04:10,061 --> 00:04:12,351
generative AI was widely used,

130
00:04:12,391 --> 00:04:14,121
and the gap shrinks when education is

131
00:04:14,161 --> 00:04:15,201
taken into account.

132
00:04:15,241 --> 00:04:17,281
Their payroll sample also shows a bigger

133
00:04:17,321 --> 00:04:19,022
gap than national surveys do.

134
00:04:19,372 --> 00:04:21,582
There is a third suspect: interest rates.

135
00:04:21,622 --> 00:04:23,002
In January 2022,

136
00:04:23,042 --> 00:04:25,342
the Federal Reserve's benchmark rate was

137
00:04:25,382 --> 00:04:26,182
close to zero,

138
00:04:26,162 --> 00:04:28,342
at 0.08 percent.

139
00:04:28,382 --> 00:04:32,042
By August 2023 it was 5.33 percent,

140
00:04:32,082 --> 00:04:33,916
and it stayed there for about a year.

141
00:04:34,036 --> 00:04:36,236
A working paper from the U.S.

142
00:04:36,276 --> 00:04:39,136
Census Bureau looks at workers aged 22 to 24.

143
00:04:39,176 --> 00:04:41,546
In the industries most exposed to AI,

144
00:04:41,586 --> 00:04:43,284
their employment fell 12 percent in the

145
00:04:43,324 --> 00:04:45,946
ten quarters after ChatGPT's release,

146
00:04:45,986 --> 00:04:48,215
while less exposed industries held steady.

147
00:04:48,335 --> 00:04:50,845
The paper then asks whether rising rates did that.

148
00:04:50,885 --> 00:04:52,505
Its estimate is that up to a quarter of

149
00:04:52,545 --> 00:04:54,025
the decline can be traced to the rate

150
00:04:54,065 --> 00:04:56,165
hikes through 2023.

151
00:04:56,205 --> 00:04:57,945
The paper finds rates can't account for

152
00:04:57,985 --> 00:05:01,236
the sharp hiring drop at the most AI-exposed firms.

153
00:05:01,586 --> 00:05:03,666
The two studies cover overlapping years,

154
00:05:03,706 --> 00:05:05,536
so this isn't a clean handoff from one

155
00:05:05,576 --> 00:05:06,736
cause to the next.

156
00:05:06,776 --> 00:05:07,969
They ask different questions.

157
00:05:08,089 --> 00:05:09,949
The New York Fed study finds the early gap

158
00:05:09,989 --> 00:05:11,479
in jobs that went remote,

159
00:05:11,519 --> 00:05:14,399
and it is still there when AI exposure is held fixed.

160
00:05:14,439 --> 00:05:15,839
The Stanford study finds a newer,

161
00:05:15,879 --> 00:05:18,739
growing gap in jobs where AI does the task,

162
00:05:18,779 --> 00:05:20,529
and it is still there when remote work is

163
00:05:20,569 --> 00:05:21,399
controlled for.

164
00:05:21,439 --> 00:05:23,299
The simplest reading is that more than one

165
00:05:23,339 --> 00:05:24,409
cause is stacking,

166
00:05:24,449 --> 00:05:27,319
and the data can't yet say how much weight each carries.

167
00:05:27,439 --> 00:05:29,019
That still leaves a graduate with two

168
00:05:29,059 --> 00:05:31,279
questions worth asking about any job.

169
00:05:31,319 --> 00:05:33,609
Will this role put me near people who can teach me?

170
00:05:33,649 --> 00:05:35,529
And is AI in this job doing the work,

171
00:05:35,569 --> 00:05:37,175
or helping someone do it?

172
00:05:37,525 --> 00:05:39,185
One problem is tied to remote work,

173
00:05:39,225 --> 00:05:41,515
and its size rests on a rough estimate.

174
00:05:41,555 --> 00:05:42,355
The other is newer,

175
00:05:42,255 --> 00:05:45,325
and it sits in jobs where AI does the task.

176
00:05:45,365 --> 00:05:47,369
How much each one matters is still open.

177
00:05:47,489 --> 00:05:49,249
Which of those two questions applies to

178
00:05:49,289 --> 00:05:50,493
your own job search?
